// A BORROW CHAIN AND A DESCENDING DIVISION, HAND-WRITTEN, on all four targets.
//
// `big-subdiv.oro` next door tests the same two algorithms as the COMPILER emits
// them, and has to skip windows: that target stores a bounded big value as limbs
// and cannot render one, because `big-str` needs a host bignum to convert
// through. So the operation reaches the one host that most needs it and nothing
// there can observe the answer.
//
// This closes that half by writing the two loops out directly and returning a
// single LIMB, which is an ordinary integer every target can print. Between the
// two cases the compiler's lowering is checked against three mature host bignums
// and the emitted loop shapes are checked on four hosts.
//
// TWO SHAPES THAT ARE OTHERWISE UNTESTED ON WINDOWS. The borrow is a conditional
// inside an `again` ARGUMENT — ADR 0015 forbids `again` under an `if`, so both
// outcomes are computed and one `again` takes them — and the division walks
// DOWNWARD, whose termination is by orientation (mu = -v) rather than by an
// ascending counter.
//
// THE OPERANDS ARE CHOSEN SO THE BORROW ACTUALLY FIRES: limb 0 of a is
// 13,008,895 and limb 0 of b is 14,000,000, so the subtraction borrows and limb 1
// of a drops from 9,272,996 to 9,272,995. A case whose borrow never fired would
// pass with the whole chain deleted.
//
//   a = 999999999999999,  b = 14000000
//   q = (a - b) / 7 = 142857140857142
//   q in base 2^24 = [2255158, 8514949, 0, …]
// ONE INPUT, AND BOTH SIGNIFICANT LIMBS IN ONE NUMBER. The harness inlines `run`
// at every call site, so three inputs is three copies of two `build`s and two
// loops — and on x86 that exceeds what the register allocator can place, which
// is a PRE-EXISTING backend ceiling (`x64.movb has more spilled operands than
// there are scratch registers`) and reproduces with this work reverted. The
// differential README already names that ceiling and says provoking it from a
// harness measures the harness, so the answer is one call whose result encodes
// what three would have checked.
//
//   2255158 + 8514949 * 100000000 = 851494902255158
// inputs: 0
// expect: 851494902255158
fn at(t, i) { if i < 0 { 0 } else if i >= len(t) { 0 } else { t[i] } }

fn bn_of(n, v) {
    var b := array { 0 : _ ∈ 0..n-1 }
    loop i = 0, r = v { i >= n -> b; else -> { b[i] := r % (2^24); again(i + 1, r / (2^24)) } }
}

fn bn_sub(n, a, b) {
    var o := array { 0 : _ ∈ 0..n-1 }
    loop i = 0, bw = 0 {
        i >= n -> o
        else -> {
            t = at(a, i) - at(b, i) - bw
            o[i] := if t < 0 { t + (2^24) } else { t }
            again(i + 1, if t < 0 { 1 } else { 0 })
        }
    }
}

fn bn_div_small(n, a, k) {
    var o := array { 0 : _ ∈ 0..n-1 }
    loop i = n - 1, r = 0 {
        i < 0 -> o
        else -> { cur = r * (2^24) + at(a, i); o[i] := cur / k; again(i - 1, cur % k) }
    }
}

export fn run(k) {
    q = bn_div_small(3, bn_sub(3, bn_of(3, 999999999999999), bn_of(3, 14000000)), 7)
    at(q, k) + at(q, k + 1) * 100000000
}
